(window.__D=window.__D||{})["data/ch02.json"]={"n": 2, "title": "Xenophon and the Ten Thousand (401–400 BC)", "events": [{"id": "H1-030", "date": "-401", "date_end": "-400", "date_text": "September 401 – autumn 400 BC", "precision": "month", "title": "The route of the Ten Thousand, Cunaxa to Byzantium", "summary": "The route of the retreat in 39 stages, as drawn on the map; for each, Xenophon's stages (stathmoi) and parasangs from the one before, how sure its location is, and whether it was reached by land or by sea. From the Kentrites to Trapezous Xenophon's stages add up to 49 stages and 257 parasangs (255 on the Loeb's reading of 4.5.3), with 8 days that he does not measure. The total at 5.5.4, generally regarded as a later addition, gives 122 stages, 620 parasangs, 18,600 stadia and eight months from the battlefield to Kotyora. Only the ends are secure. Most points between the Kentrites and Trapezous are conjectural; for Theches the map follows Mitford (2000), the leading proposal.", "kind": "route", "people": ["Xenophon", "Cheirisophos", "Klearchos", "Tissaphernes"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anabasis 1.8–7.1", "Xenophon, Anab. 5.5.4 (totals to Kotyora; generally regarded as an interpolation)", "Anab. 7.8.26 (grand totals; probably interpolated)", "Diodorus 14.25–31"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995, Kommentar zu Xenophons Anabasis", "Manfredi 1986, La strada dei Diecimila", "Mitford 2000; Mitford 2018", "Brennan & Tuplin 2023, Anatolian Studies 73: 129–149", "Lee 2007, A Greek Army on the March", "Waterfield 2006, Xenophon's Retreat"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Nearly every point between the Tigris and Trapezous is disputed: the Teleboas, the 'Euphrates' crossing, the Phasis (now usually the upper Araxes), the Harpasos (usually the Çoruh), Gymnias and Theches. The parasang is nominally 30 stadia (c. 5.5–6 km), but Xenophon's figures shrink in the mountains (3.4 parasangs a day from Theches to Trapezous).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Cunaxa", "lat": 33.2, "lon": 44.05, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 1: battlefield, start of the retreat"}, {"name": "Sittake (bridge of 37 boats over the Tigris)", "lat": 33.22, "lon": 44.48, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Opis on the Physkos", "lat": 34.05, "lon": 44.2, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Villages of Parysatis", "lat": 34.75, "lon": 43.7, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Opposite Kainai (Assur?)", "lat": 35.46, "lon": 43.29, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Zapatas (Great Zab) camp; the generals seized", "lat": 35.99, "lon": 43.37, "certainty": "probable", "role": "waypoint 6"}, {"name": "Larisa (Nimrud)", "lat": 36.098, "lon": 43.329, "certainty": "secure", "role": "waypoint 7"}, {"name": "Mespila (Nineveh)", "lat": 36.359, "lon": 43.153, "certainty": "secure", "role": "waypoint 8"}, {"name": "Edge of the Kardouchian mountains", "lat": 37.08, "lon": 42.4, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 9"}, {"name": "Kentrites (Bohtan Su) crossing", "lat": 37.88, "lon": 42.05, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 10: into Armenia"}, {"name": "Village with a satrap's palace", "lat": 38.15, "lon": 42.1, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 11"}, {"name": "Sources of the Tigris (passed)", "lat": 38.4, "lon": 42.11, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 12"}, {"name": "Teleboas river", "lat": 38.68, "lon": 41.55, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 13: Western Armenia, truce with Tiribazos"}, {"name": "Tiribazos' palace and villages", "lat": 38.9, "lon": 42.0, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 14"}, {"name": "Euphrates (Murat) crossing", "lat": 39.15, "lon": 42.5, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 15"}, {"name": "Underground villages (the komarch)", "lat": 39.38, "lon": 42.0, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 16"}, {"name": "Phasis (upper Araxes, Pasin plain)", "lat": 39.97, "lon": 41.86, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 17"}, {"name": "Pass held by Chalybes, Taochoi and Phasianoi", "lat": 40.17, "lon": 41.88, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 18"}, {"name": "Taochoi stronghold", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 42.05, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 19"}, {"name": "Harpasos (Çoruh) crossing", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 41.2, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 20"}, {"name": "Villages of the Skythenoi", "lat": 40.42, "lon": 40.75, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 21"}, {"name": "Gymnias (Bayburt?)", "lat": 40.258, "lon": 40.227, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 22"}, {"name": "Patara (Maden hanları), on the Roman summer road", "lat": 40.56, "lon": 39.66, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 23 (Mitford variant)"}, {"name": "Mount Theches (Mitford's site, c. 20 km SSW of Maçka)", "lat": 40.645, "lon": 39.54, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 24: 'Thalatta, thalatta'"}, {"name": "River between the Makrones and the Skythenoi (near Maçka)", "lat": 40.81, "lon": 39.61, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 25"}, {"name": "Colchian mountain (battle)", "lat": 40.9, "lon": 39.66, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 26"}, {"name": "Colchian villages of the mad honey", "lat": 40.94, "lon": 39.69, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 27"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.004, "lon": 39.722, "certainty": "secure", "role": "waypoint 28: the sea; 30 days' stay"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Xenophon's, east of Vakfıkebir)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 39.33, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 29: review of 8,600 men"}, {"name": "Mossynoikoi 'metropolis'", "lat": 40.98, "lon": 38.8, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 30"}, {"name": "Chalybes (coastal iron-workers)", "lat": 40.93, "lon": 38.25, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 31"}, {"name": "Tibarenoi", "lat": 40.97, "lon": 38.05, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "waypoint 32"}, {"name": "Kotyora (Ordu)", "lat": 40.985, "lon": 37.879, "certainty": "probable", "role": "waypoint 33: 45 days' stay"}, {"name": "Jason's Cape (Yason Burnu), passed by sea", "lat": 41.11, "lon": 37.68, "certainty": "probable", "role": "waypoint 34"}, {"name": "Harmene, harbour of Sinope", "lat": 42.04, "lon": 35.06, "certainty": "probable", "role": "waypoint 35: 5 days' stay"}, {"name": "Heraclea Pontica", "lat": 41.283, "lon": 31.418, "certainty": "secure", "role": "waypoint 36: the army splits in three"}, {"name": "Kalpe Harbour (Kerpe)", "lat": 41.165, "lon": 30.215, "certainty": "probable", "role": "waypoint 37: the army reunited"}, {"name": "Chrysopolis (Üsküdar)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 29.015, "certainty": "secure", "role": "waypoint 38"}, {"name": "Byzantium", "lat": 41.01, "lon": 28.982, "certainty": "secure", "role": "waypoint 39: end of the retreat"}]}, {"id": "H1-031", "date": "-401", "date_text": "September 401 BC (exact day unknown)", "precision": "month", "title": "Cunaxa: Cyrus is killed", "summary": "At Cunaxa on the Euphrates, about 500 stadia above Babylon, Cyrus the Younger met his brother Artaxerxes II. The Greek mercenaries (10,400 hoplites and 2,500 peltasts, 1.7.10) routed the wing facing them, but Cyrus was killed charging the King. The Greeks were left victorious on their own wing, stranded in the heart of the empire, more than 1,500 km from the sea.", "kind": "event", "people": ["Cyrus the Younger", "Artaxerxes II", "Klearchos", "Tissaphernes"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 1.7.10, 1.8–1.10", "Plutarch, Artaxerxes 8–11 (with Ctesias)", "Diodorus 14.22–24"], "sources_modern": ["Waterfield 2006", "Lee 2007", "Bigwood 1983, 'The ancient accounts of the battle of Cunaxa', AJPh 104"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The day is often given as 3 September 401, a modern reckoning. The battlefield has not been found (Tell Kuneise has been proposed).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Cunaxa", "lat": 33.2, "lon": 44.05, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "battlefield"}, {"name": "Babylon", "lat": 32.542, "lon": 44.421, "role": "the King's capital, Cyrus' goal"}]}, {"id": "H1-032", "date": "-401", "date_text": "October 401 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "The generals seized; Xenophon elected", "summary": "The Greeks marched north with Tissaphernes, crossing the Tigris at Sittake on a bridge of 37 boats and passing Opis and Kainai. At the Great Zab (Zapatas) Tissaphernes invited Klearchos and four other generals to a parley and had them arrested and later executed. That night the young Athenian Xenophon roused the officers. New generals were elected, among them Xenophon and the Spartan Cheirisophos, and the army chose to fight its way north (3.1–3.2).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Klearchos", "Proxenos", "Menon", "Tissaphernes", "Xenophon", "Cheirisophos"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 2.4–3.2", "Diodorus 14.26–27", "Ctesias (in Plutarch, Artaxerxes 18)"], "sources_modern": ["Waterfield 2006", "Lee 2007"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The sites of Sittake, Opis and Kainai are uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sittake", "lat": 33.22, "lon": 44.48, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "Tigris crossing"}, {"name": "Opis on the Physkos", "lat": 34.05, "lon": 44.2, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "city passed"}, {"name": "Opposite Kainai", "lat": 35.46, "lon": 43.29, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "city across the Tigris"}, {"name": "Zapatas camp", "lat": 35.99, "lon": 43.37, "certainty": "probable", "role": "the generals seized"}]}, {"id": "H1-033", "date": "-401", "date_text": "October–November 401 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Up the Tigris: Larisa and Mespila", "summary": "Harried by Persian cavalry and slingers, the Greeks marched up the east bank of the Tigris. They passed two deserted cities that Xenophon calls Larisa and Mespila, once held by the Medes: in fact the Assyrian capitals of Nimrud and Nineveh, ruined two centuries earlier. Where the Kardouchian mountains came down to the river they turned north into the mountains.", "kind": "event", "people": ["Tissaphernes", "Xenophon"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 3.3–3.5"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995", "Waterfield 2006"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Where exactly the army left the Tigris is uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Larisa (Nimrud)", "lat": 36.098, "lon": 43.329, "role": "ruined city"}, {"name": "Mespila (Nineveh)", "lat": 36.359, "lon": 43.153, "role": "ruined city"}, {"name": "Edge of the Kardouchian mountains", "lat": 37.08, "lon": 42.4, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "turn into the mountains"}]}, {"id": "H1-034", "date": "-401", "date_text": "November 401 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Seven days through the Kardouchoi", "summary": "The Kardouchoi (in the mountains of today's Hakkari and Şırnak) were independent of the King, and Xenophon says they had once destroyed a royal army of 120,000. For seven days they fought the Greeks in narrow defiles, rolling stones and shooting arrows from bows nearly three cubits long. The Greeks 'suffered more than from the King and Tissaphernes together' (4.3.2).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Xenophon", "Cheirisophos"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.1–4.3.2"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995", "Lee 2007"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The exact route through the mountains is unknown.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kardouchian mountains", "lat": 37.5, "lon": 42.3, "certainty": "approximate", "role": "seven days' fighting"}]}, {"id": "H1-035", "date": "-401", "date_text": "Late November–December 401 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Crossing the Kentrites into Armenia", "summary": "At the Kentrites (Bohtan Su), about 2 plethra wide, the boundary between the Kardouchoi and Armenia, the satrap Orontas and Artouchas held the far bank with Armenians, Mardoi and Chaldaioi mercenaries, while the Kardouchoi pressed from behind. Two young soldiers found a ford, and the army crossed in a deceptive double movement (4.3.8–34).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Orontas", "Artouchas", "Xenophon", "Cheirisophos"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.3"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The crossing point on the Bohtan is not known.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kentrites (Bohtan Su) crossing", "lat": 37.88, "lon": 42.05, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "river crossing"}]}, {"id": "H1-036", "date": "-401", "date_text": "December 401 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Western Armenia: the Teleboas and the truce with Tiribazos", "summary": "Beyond the Kentrites they marched 1 stage of 5 parasangs to a village with a satrap's palace, 2 stages of 10 parasangs past 'the sources of the Tigris', and 3 stages of 15 parasangs to the Teleboas, 'a beautiful river, though not a large one' (4.4.1–3). This was Western Armenia, under Tiribazos, the King's 'friend', who alone was allowed to help him mount his horse. He made a truce: the Greeks could take supplies if they burned nothing (4.4.4–6).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Tiribazos"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.4.1–6"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995", "Manfredi 1986"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Teleboas is usually taken as the Kara Su of the Muş plain; some take it as a stretch of the Murat.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Village with a satrap's palace", "lat": 38.15, "lon": 42.1, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "first stop in Armenia"}, {"name": "Sources of the Tigris", "lat": 38.4, "lon": 42.11, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "passed"}, {"name": "Teleboas river", "lat": 38.68, "lon": 41.55, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "truce with Tiribazos"}]}, {"id": "H1-037", "date": "-401", "date_text": "December 401 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Snow, and Tiribazos' camp taken", "summary": "After 3 more stages (15 parasangs) to a palace among villages, heavy snow fell at night. A captured scout revealed that Tiribazos meant to ambush the Greeks in a pass. They struck first, took his camp with his silver-footed couch and his cup-bearers, and crossed the pass (4.4.7–22).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Tiribazos"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.4.7–22"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tiribazos' palace and villages", "lat": 38.9, "lon": 42.0, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "snowbound camp"}]}, {"id": "H1-038", "date": "-401", "date_text": "December 401 – January 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Crossing the Euphrates near its sources", "summary": "After three stages (15 parasangs) 'through desert country' the army waded the Euphrates, wetted to the navel, and was told its sources were not far away (4.5.2). This was probably the Murat, the eastern Euphrates.", "kind": "event", "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.5.1–2 (4.5.2: 3 stages, 15 parasangs)"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995", "Manfredi 1986"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Which branch of the Euphrates was crossed, and where, is uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Euphrates (Murat) crossing", "lat": 39.15, "lon": 42.5, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "ford"}]}, {"id": "H1-039", "date": "-401", "date_end": "-400", "date_text": "December 401 – January 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "The snow march", "summary": "Then came three stages through deep snow (15 parasangs in Marchant's OCT; 13 in Brownson's Loeb). On the third a north wind blew in their faces and froze the army; the seers sacrificed to the wind, and it seemed to drop. The snow lay a fathom deep (c. 1.8 m); many pack animals and slaves died, and about 30 soldiers. Men went snow-blind and lost toes to frostbite, and Xenophon drove stragglers on in the rearguard (4.5.3–22).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Xenophon"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.5.3–22", "Diodorus 14.28"], "sources_modern": ["Lee 2007 (cold injuries and survival)", "Lendle 1995"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The parasangs of this leg are 15 in Marchant's Oxford text and 13 in Brownson's Loeb, a difference between the manuscripts.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Snow march (between the Murat and the villages)", "lat": 39.27, "lon": 42.25, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "three stages in deep snow"}]}, {"id": "H1-040", "date": "-400", "date_text": "January 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "The underground villages of Armenia", "summary": "The army rested about a week in villages where the houses were dug into the ground, with entrances like wells, and men and animals lived together. They drank barley wine through reeds from great bowls. Xenophon lodged with the village chief (komarch), whose daughter had been married nine days before, and who was rearing colts as tribute for the King. Taken as a guide, the chief led them for three days, until Cheirisophos struck him for not finding villages; he slipped away in the night (4.5.23–4.6.3).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Xenophon", "Cheirisophos", "the komarch", "Episthenes of Amphipolis"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.5.23–36, 4.6.1–3"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995", "Lane Fox (ed.) 2004"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Where the villages were is unknown; the Hınıs–Karaçoban region is a guess.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Underground villages", "lat": 39.38, "lon": 42.0, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "a week's rest"}]}, {"id": "H1-041", "date": "-400", "date_text": "January–February 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Seven stages to the Phasis", "summary": "Then came 7 stages at 5 parasangs a day to the river Phasis, a plethron wide (4.6.4). This was not the Phasis of Colchis but, almost certainly, the upper Araxes (Aras) in the Pasin plain, whose people Xenophon calls Phasianoi.", "kind": "event", "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.6.4"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995", "Manfredi 1986"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Older scholars took it for the Phasis of Colchis; the upper Araxes is now the accepted identification.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Phasis (upper Araxes) at the Pasin plain", "lat": 39.97, "lon": 41.86, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "river reached"}]}, {"id": "H1-042", "date": "-400", "date_text": "February 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Forcing the pass of the Chalybes, Taochoi and Phasianoi", "summary": "Two stages (10 parasangs) on, the Chalybes, Taochoi and Phasianoi held the pass down to the next plain. Xenophon and Cheirisophos joked about whether Spartans or Athenians were better thieves. Then a night detachment seized a height on the flank, the defenders fled, and the Greeks came down to well-stocked villages (4.6.5–27).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Xenophon", "Cheirisophos"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.6.5–27"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The pass lay north of the Pasin plain; its exact place is unknown.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Pass held by Chalybes, Taochoi and Phasianoi", "lat": 40.17, "lon": 41.88, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "forced pass"}]}, {"id": "H1-043", "date": "-400", "date_text": "February 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "The Taochoi stronghold", "summary": "After 5 stages (30 parasangs) into the land of the Taochoi, food ran out. The Taochoi had gathered their families and herds in a stronghold on a cliff. When the Greeks broke in, women threw their children and then themselves over the edge, and Aeneas of Stymphalus, grabbing a man who was about to jump, was dragged over with him (4.7.1–14).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Aeneas of Stymphalus", "Agasias of Stymphalus", "Kallimachos of Parrhasia"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.7.1–14"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995", "Lane Fox (ed.) 2004"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The stronghold lay in the Tao country (Oltu–Tortum); its exact site is unknown.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Taochoi stronghold", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 42.05, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "stormed refuge"}]}, {"id": "H1-044", "date": "-400", "date_text": "February 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Through the Chalybes", "summary": "Seven stages (50 parasangs) led through the Chalybes, 'the bravest of all the peoples they passed'. They wore linen corselets, greaves and helmets, and carried a curved knife like the Spartan's and a spear 15 cubits long. They cut off the heads of the dead and lived in strongholds, from which they harassed the column (4.7.15–17).", "kind": "event", "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.7.15–17"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Fifty parasangs is a long way for seven stages, which suggests a detour or a fault in the text.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chalybes territory", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 41.4, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "seven stages of fighting"}]}, {"id": "H1-045", "date": "-400", "date_text": "February–March 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "The Harpasos and the Skythenoi", "summary": "They reached the river Harpasos, 4 plethra wide, and then marched 4 stages (20 parasangs) through the level land of the Skythenoi to villages, where they rested three days (4.7.18).", "kind": "event", "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.7.18"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995", "Manfredi 1986"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Harpasos is usually taken as the upper Çoruh (Akampsis); others suggest a tributary.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Harpasos (Çoruh) crossing", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 41.2, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "river"}, {"name": "Villages of the Skythenoi", "lat": 40.42, "lon": 40.75, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "three days' rest"}]}, {"id": "H1-046", "date": "-400", "date_text": "March 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Gymnias", "summary": "Four more stages (20 parasangs) brought them to 'a large, prosperous, inhabited city called Gymnias'. Its ruler sent a guide to lead them through the land of his enemies. The guide promised that within five days he would bring them to a place from which they could see the sea, or they could put him to death (4.7.19–20).", "kind": "event", "people": ["the ruler of Gymnias", "the guide"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.7.19–20", "Diodorus 14.29.3"], "sources_modern": ["Brennan & Tuplin 2023 ('the normal view' is the Gümüşhane–Bayburt region)", "Lendle 1995", "Mitford 2000", "Paradeisopoulos 2013, 2014 (Gymnias far to the east, in Armenia)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "Gymnias is usually placed at Bayburt, or near Gümüşhane; Paradeisopoulos (2013, Greek) argues from Diodorus' '15 days' for a site far to the east, in Armenia. The guide led the army through the land of his own enemies, so that the Greeks served as his weapon.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Gymnias, candidate: Bayburt", "lat": 40.258, "lon": 40.227, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "majority view"}, {"name": "Gymnias, candidate: Gümüşhane area", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "alternative"}]}, {"id": "H1-047", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Late winter or spring 400 BC (estimates run from February to May)", "precision": "month", "title": "Mount Theches: 'Thalatta, thalatta!'", "summary": "On the fifth day they reached a mountain called Theches. The vanguard shouted, the shout spread, and Xenophon rode up expecting an attack, then heard 'The sea! The sea!'. Men embraced their generals and captains in tears. They built a great cairn of stones and laid on it raw ox-hides, sticks and captured wicker shields. They gave the guide a horse, a silver bowl, a Persian robe and ten darics; he asked above all for rings and got many, showed them a village to camp in, and left at night (4.7.21–27). The place has never been identified with certainty.", "kind": "event", "people": ["Xenophon", "the guide"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.7.21–27", "Diodorus 14.29.3–4 (mountain 'Chenion', 15 days from Gymnias)", "Arrian, Periplus 1.1–4 (Arrian views the sea at Trapezous 'from the same spot' as Xenophon)"], "sources_modern": ["Brennan & Tuplin 2023, 'Theches: an elusive mountain', Anatolian Studies 73: 129–149", "Mitford 2000, 'Thalatta, thalatta: Xenophon's view of the Black Sea', Anatolian Studies 50: 127–131", "Mitford 2018, East of Asia Minor (OUP)", "Manfredi 1986, La strada dei Diecimila, pp. 223–228", "Manfredi 2004, 'The identification of Mount Thekes...', in Tuplin (ed.), Xenophon and his World, pp. 319–324", "Lendle 1995, pp. 272–280", "Rood 2004, The Sea! The Sea!", "Waterfield 2006", "Lane Fox (ed.) 2004, The Long March"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "Brennan & Tuplin (2023) count about twenty proposals since 1818 and call Mitford's site 'the current leading contender'; their own 'viable hypothesis' is the north-west spur of Polut Dağı, but they conclude that the spot 'must remain a matter for conjecture'. Diodorus' 'Chenion' and '15 days' are usually treated as copying errors. Arrian says that he looked at the sea at Trapezous 'from the same spot' as Xenophon, but this is not taken as evidence for the site.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Theches, candidate: Mitford's site (Kolat area, c. 20 km SSW of Maçka)", "lat": 40.645, "lon": 39.54, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "Mitford 2000, 2018; Manfredi 2004 (after earlier proposing Zigana); reported in 1997 as 'Deveboynu Tepe' with a cairn base c. 40 ft wide"}, {"name": "Theches, candidate: east of the Zigana pass", "lat": 40.63, "lon": 39.45, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "Lendle 1995 (c. 4 km E of the pass); Chesney 1850; Hoffmeister 1911; Segl 1925; Lehmann-Haupt 1926; Gassner 1953"}, {"name": "Theches, candidate: south of Toplukdüzü Tepe, NW of Torul", "lat": 40.62, "lon": 39.21, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "Manfredi 1986"}, {"name": "Theches, candidate: İskobel", "lat": 40.685, "lon": 39.691, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "local tradition (triangulation point)"}, {"name": "Theches, candidate: Polut Dağı north-west spur (2,315 m)", "lat": 40.673, "lon": 39.963, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "Brennan & Tuplin 2023 ('viable', not demonstrable)"}, {"name": "Theches, candidate: Madur Dağı (2,742 m)", "lat": 40.652, "lon": 40.041, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "Bilgin 2000; also Winfield 1977, Brennan 2012"}, {"name": "Theches, candidate: Vavuk pass (old proposal)", "lat": 40.32, "lon": 39.93, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "Briot; Lynch; Boucher 1913"}, {"name": "Theches, candidate: Kop Dağı (old proposal)", "lat": 40.03, "lon": 40.49, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "Ainsworth 1844"}]}, {"id": "H1-048", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Late winter or spring 400 BC", "precision": "month", "title": "Theches: the alternative routes from Gymnias to Trapezous", "summary": "The map shows a route from Gymnias (Bayburt) to Trapezous for each of the three main proposals for Theches: by Mitford's site south-south-west of Maçka (the route shown by default), by the Zigana pass (Lendle), and by the north-west spur of Polut Dağı (Brennan & Tuplin).", "kind": "route", "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.7.19–4.8.22"], "sources_modern": ["Mitford, T. 2000, 'Thalatta, thalatta: Xenophon's view of the Black Sea', Anatolian Studies 50: 127–132 (route Bayburt – Kale (Kovans) – Son Kale – Maden Hanları – Anzarya Hanları – Kolat Hanları; the sea first seen from a plateau about a mile north-west of Kolat on the Roman road, c. 8,250 ft; Theches 'presumably' Deveboynu Tepe, c. 10,100 ft, 'the highest summit between Bayburt and Trabzon'; descent by Turnagöl, Karakaban and Meşecik han to Maçka)", "Lendle 1995, pp. 272–280 and map 49", "Tuplin, C. & Brennan, S. 2023, 'Theches: an elusive mountain', Anatolian Studies 73: 129–149, doi 10.1017/S0066154623000054"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The routes are reconstructions. Mitford (2000) traces his from Bayburt by Maden Hanları and Kolat Hanları, where the sea is first seen from a plateau on the Roman road, down to Maçka, but gives no coordinates for his viewpoint or for Deveboynu Tepe, so those points are approximate. The descent of the eastern route by the Karadere valley to Araklı is inferred.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Gymnias (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.258, "lon": 40.227, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant A (Mitford), waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Maden Hanları", "lat": 40.56, "lon": 39.66, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant A (Mitford), waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Theches (Mitford's site)", "lat": 40.645, "lon": 39.54, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant A (Mitford), waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Maçka (Değirmendere–Sumela junction)", "lat": 40.813, "lon": 39.613, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant A (Mitford), waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.004, "lon": 39.722, "role": "variant A (Mitford), waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Gymnias (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.258, "lon": 40.227, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant B (Zigana: Lendle), waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Vavuk pass", "lat": 40.32, "lon": 39.93, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant B (Zigana: Lendle), waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant B (Zigana: Lendle), waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Theches (c. 4 km E of the Zigana pass)", "lat": 40.63, "lon": 39.45, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant B (Zigana: Lendle), waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Hamsiköy (boundary stream)", "lat": 40.69, "lon": 39.465, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant B (Zigana: Lendle), waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Maçka", "lat": 40.813, "lon": 39.613, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant B (Zigana: Lendle), waypoint 6"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.004, "lon": 39.722, "role": "variant B (Zigana: Lendle), waypoint 7"}, {"name": "Gymnias (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.258, "lon": 40.227, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant C (east: Brennan & Tuplin), waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Theches (Polut Dağı NW spur)", "lat": 40.673, "lon": 39.963, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant C (east: Brennan & Tuplin), waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Karadere confluence (boundary river?)", "lat": 40.8, "lon": 40.03, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant C (east: Brennan & Tuplin), waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Coast near Araklı", "lat": 40.94, "lon": 40.06, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "variant C (east: Brennan & Tuplin), waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.004, "lon": 39.722, "role": "variant C (east: Brennan & Tuplin), waypoint 5"}]}, {"id": "H1-049", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Spring 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Through the Makrones", "summary": "The march through the Makrones took 3 stages (10 parasangs); on the first day the army came to the river between the Makrones and the Skythenoi, where the Makrones blocked the crossing. A peltast who had been a slave at Athens recognised their language and could talk to them. Spears were exchanged as pledges, the Makrones helped cut a road, and they brought the Greeks in three days to the Colchian border (4.8.1–8).", "kind": "event", "people": ["unnamed peltast (former slave at Athens)"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.8.1–8"], "sources_modern": ["Brennan & Tuplin 2023 (the river's candidates by variant)", "Lendle 1995 (stream near Hamsiköy)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The river depends on the route: on Mitford's it is at the junction of the Değirmendere and the Sumela stream near Maçka, on Lendle's a stream north-north-east of Hamsiköy, on the Polut route the Karadere confluence.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "River between the Makrones and the Skythenoi", "lat": 40.81, "lon": 39.61, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "boundary river (variant-dependent)"}]}, {"id": "H1-050", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Spring 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Battle on the Colchian mountain", "summary": "The Colchians held a high mountain on the way down to the sea. Xenophon formed the hoplites into companies in column (orthioi lochoi), about eighty of them, each of about 100 men (4.8.15), with gaps between them, so that the line could cross broken ground without breaking up. The Greeks overlapped the Colchian line at both ends, and the defenders fled (4.8.9–19).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Xenophon", "Cheirisophos"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.8.9–19"], "sources_modern": ["Lee 2007", "Lendle 1995"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Colchian mountain", "lat": 40.9, "lon": 39.66, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "battle"}]}, {"id": "H1-051", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Spring 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "The mad honey", "summary": "In the Colchian villages beyond the mountain there were many beehives. Every soldier who ate the honey lost his senses, vomited and had diarrhoea, and none could stand. Those who ate a little were like drunk men; those who ate much were like madmen, and some like dying men. They lay about as if after a defeat. No one died, and about the same hour next day they came round; on the third and fourth days they got up as if after a drugging (4.8.20–21). The cause is grayanotoxin in honey from rhododendrons (Rhododendron ponticum and luteum), still known locally as deli bal.", "kind": "event", "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.8.20–21", "Diodorus 14.30.1–2", "Strabo 12.3.18 (the Heptakometai use honey against Pompey's men)", "Stephanus of Byzantium s.v. Τραπεζοῦς (citing Aristotle on maddening boxwood honey at Trapezous)", "Pliny, NH 21.77 (the Sanni's 'maenomenon' honey, from rhododendron flowers)"], "sources_modern": ["Mayor 2003, Greek Fire, Poison Arrows and Scorpion Bombs", "Jansen et al. 2012, Cardiovascular Toxicology 12: 208–215"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Colchian villages of the honey", "lat": 40.94, "lon": 39.69, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "where the honey was eaten"}]}, {"id": "H1-052", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Spring 400 BC (estimate; c. 30 days)", "precision": "month", "title": "Trapezous: a month among the Colchian villages", "summary": "Two stages (7 parasangs) later they reached the sea at Trapezous, 'an inhabited Greek city (πόλιν Ἑλληνίδα οἰκουμένην) on the Euxine Sea, a colony of the Sinopeans in the land of the Colchians' (4.8.22). This is the first certain record of the city. The army camped for about 30 days in Colchian villages and plundered Colchian land from there. The Trapezountines provided a market and gave gifts of oxen, barley meal and wine, and spoke for their Colchian neighbours in the plain, who also sent oxen (4.8.22–24).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Xenophon", "Cheirisophos"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.8.22–24", "Diodorus 14.30.3"], "sources_modern": ["Avram, Hind & Tsetskhladze 2004, no. 734", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 178–250 (Trebizond)", "Mitford 2018, vol. 1, pp. 385–425"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.004, "lon": 39.722, "role": "Greek city reached"}, {"name": "Colchian villages around Trapezous", "lat": 40.95, "lon": 39.85, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "camp for 30 days"}]}, {"id": "H1-053", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Spring 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "The games at Trapezous", "summary": "The army sacrificed the oxen it had been given to Zeus the Saviour and to Herakles the Guide, as it had vowed, and held games on the hill where it camped. The organiser was Drakontios, a Spartan exiled as a boy for accidentally killing another boy with a dagger. The events were a stadion race (mostly captive boys), a long race with more than 60 Cretans, wrestling, boxing and the pankration, and a horse race down the steep slope into the sea and back up to the altar, amid much shouting and laughter (4.8.25–28).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Drakontios of Sparta"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 4.8.25–28"], "sources_modern": ["Lee 2007", "Lane Fox (ed.) 2004"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Which hill is not known; Boztepe above the city is the usual guess.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (hill of the games)", "lat": 41.004, "lon": 39.722, "role": "games"}]}, {"id": "H1-054", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Spring 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Waiting for ships", "summary": "Leon of Thurii spoke for the army: he was tired of packing, marching, running, carrying arms, keeping in line, standing guard and fighting, and wanted to finish the journey stretched out asleep, like Odysseus. Cheirisophos sailed to the Spartan admiral Anaxibios at Byzantium for ships. Xenophon borrowed a penteconter from Trapezous for the Laconian Dexippos, who deserted with it, and a triaconter for the Athenian Polykrates, who brought in passing merchant ships (5.1).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Leon of Thurii", "Cheirisophos", "Anaxibios", "Dexippos", "Polykrates"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 5.1"], "sources_modern": ["Lee 2007"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.004, "lon": 39.722, "role": "army in camp"}, {"name": "Byzantium", "lat": 41.01, "lon": 28.982, "role": "Anaxibios' base"}]}, {"id": "H1-055", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Spring 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "The raid on the Drilai", "summary": "The Trapezountines led half the army against the Drilai, the most warlike people of the Pontus, rather than against the Colchians, who were their friends. The Drilai burned their lowland villages and withdrew to a fortified 'metropolis' with a ditch, palisade and wooden towers. The Greeks broke in, plundered, and fought their way out under cover of fire (5.2). Arrian later thought the Drilai were the Sannoi (Periplus 11).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Xenophon"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 5.2", "Arrian, Periplus 11 (Roos)"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Where the Drilai lived is uncertain; south-east of Trapezous is likely if they were the Sannoi.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Drilai country", "lat": 40.85, "lon": 39.98, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "target of the raid"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.004, "lon": 39.722, "role": "base"}]}, {"id": "H1-056", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Spring 400 BC (estimate; 10 days)", "precision": "month", "title": "Kerasous: review of the army and the tithe", "summary": "The sick, those over forty, the women and children and the spare baggage went by ship with the oldest generals, Philesios and Sophainetos; the rest marched by a well-made road and reached Kerasous on the third day. Kerasous was a Greek city by the sea, a colony of Sinope in Colchian land (5.3.2). They stayed ten days. A review under arms counted 8,600 men, and the rest had died by enemies, snow or sickness. The proceeds from the sale of captives were divided, and a tenth set aside for Apollo and for Artemis of Ephesus. Xenophon later used his share for Artemis to buy an estate and shrine at Skillous near Olympia (5.3.4–13).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Philesios", "Sophainetos", "Xenophon", "Megabyzos (Artemis' warden)"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 5.3.1–13", "Diodorus 14.30.5"], "sources_modern": ["Erciyas 2007"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Xenophon's Kerasous was not Giresun, which became Pharnakeia, but a harbour three days west of Trapezous; its exact site is unknown.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Xenophon's, east of Vakfıkebir)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 39.33, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "review and tithe"}, {"name": "Ephesus", "lat": 37.94, "lon": 27.34, "role": "Artemis' sanctuary"}, {"name": "Delphi", "lat": 38.482, "lon": 22.501, "role": "Apollo's tithe"}, {"name": "Skillous", "lat": 37.64, "lon": 21.69, "certainty": "approximate", "role": "Xenophon's later estate"}]}, {"id": "H1-057", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Spring 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Among the Mossynoikoi", "summary": "The Trapezountine Timesitheos, proxenos of the Mossynoikoi, interpreted. The Greeks allied with one faction of the Mossynoikoi, who came in 300 dugout canoes, against the other, and captured their 'metropolis'. The king, who lived in a wooden tower (mossyn), refused to come out and was burned in it. The children of the rich were fattened on boiled chestnuts until they were nearly as broad as tall, and tattooed with flowers. Xenophon calls the Mossynoikoi the most barbarous people they passed, doing in public what others do in private (5.4).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Timesitheos of Trapezous"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 5.4", "Apollonius, Arg. 2.1015–1029", "Diodorus 14.30.6–7"], "sources_modern": ["Lendle 1995", "Lane Fox (ed.) 2004 (on Xenophon's ethnography)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Location: Xenophon's Mossynoikoi lived on the coast west of his Kerasous; Strabo places them inland near Trapezous.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Mossynoikoi 'metropolis'", "lat": 40.98, "lon": 38.8, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "captured stronghold"}]}, {"id": "H1-058", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Late spring 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Chalybes and Tibarenoi to Kotyora: the end of the land march", "summary": "After 8 stages through the Mossynoikoi came the Chalybes, few, subject to the Mossynoikoi, and living mostly by iron-working. Then the Tibarenoi, whose land was flatter and their coastal forts weaker; the generals wanted to plunder them, but the omens forbade it, and the Tibarenoi sent gifts. After 2 stages through Tibarenian land they reached Kotyora, a colony of Sinope (5.5.1–3). A summary at 5.5.4, generally regarded as an interpolation, gives from the battlefield to Kotyora 122 stages, 620 parasangs, 18,600 stadia and 8 months.", "kind": "event", "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 5.5.1–4"], "sources_modern": ["Erciyas 2007", "Lendle 1995"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Kotyora is probably Ordu.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chalybes (coastal)", "lat": 40.93, "lon": 38.25, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "iron-workers"}, {"name": "Tibarenoi", "lat": 40.97, "lon": 38.05, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "spared after gifts"}, {"name": "Kotyora (Ordu)", "lat": 40.985, "lon": 37.879, "certainty": "probable", "role": "end of the land march"}]}, {"id": "H1-059", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Early summer 400 BC (estimate; 45 days)", "precision": "month", "title": "Forty-five days at Kotyora; Sinope's envoys; Xenophon's colony plan", "summary": "Kotyora would not open a market or take the sick inside its walls. The army took supplies from Kotyorite and Paphlagonian land and stayed 45 days, with sacrifices and games. Sinope, which drew tribute from Kotyora and had a governor (harmost) there (and, Hekatonymos says, tribute from Kerasous and Trapezous too, 5.5.10), sent envoys led by Hekatonymos, who threatened an alliance with Korylas of Paphlagonia. He then advised going by sea, describing Paphlagonia's cavalry and its rivers: Thermodon, Iris, Halys, Parthenios. Xenophon thought of founding a city on the Pontus with the army. The seer Silanos betrayed the plan, Timasion and Thorax stirred opposition, and the generals were tried in an assembly (5.5.5–5.8).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Hekatonymos of Sinope", "Korylas", "Xenophon", "Silanos of Ambracia", "Timasion of Dardanos", "Thorax of Boeotia"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 5.5.5–5.8"], "sources_modern": ["Lane Fox (ed.) 2004 (on the colony plan)", "Lee 2007"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Xenophon says only that he meant to found his city on the Pontus (5.6.15–16).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kotyora (Ordu)", "lat": 40.985, "lon": 37.879, "certainty": "probable", "role": "45 days' stay"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "envoys"}, {"name": "Paphlagonia (Korylas)", "lat": 41.4, "lon": 34.0, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "threatened alliance"}]}, {"id": "H1-060", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Summer 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "By sea to Sinope; Cheirisophos made sole commander", "summary": "Envoys from Korylas were feasted with armed dances: the Thracian sword dance, the Karpaia of the Ainianes and Magnetes, a Mysian shield dance, the Mantineans' war dance, and a girl dancing the pyrrhic (6.1.1–13). Ships came, and the army sailed a day and a night with a fair wind and anchored next day at Harmene, the harbour of Sinope. The Sinopeans, 'Milesian colonists in Paphlagonia', sent 3,000 medimnoi of barley meal and 1,500 jars of wine. Cheirisophos returned, bringing only praise from Anaxibios. After five days the army chose a single commander: Xenophon declined after the omens, and Cheirisophos was elected (6.1.14–33).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Korylas", "Cheirisophos", "Xenophon", "Anaxibios"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 6.1"], "sources_modern": ["Lee 2007"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Harmene (harbour of Sinope)", "lat": 42.04, "lon": 35.06, "certainty": "probable", "role": "anchorage, 5 days"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "supplies"}]}, {"id": "H1-061", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Summer 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Heraclea: the army splits in three", "summary": "From Harmene the army sailed two days with a fair wind, past the mouth of the Parthenios, to Heraclea, a Megarian colony in Mariandynian land (6.2.1). The text then lists sights of the voyage (Jason's Cape, where the Argo was said to have anchored, and the mouths of the Thermodon, Iris, Halys and Parthenios), a list Brownson brackets as an interpolation. They anchored by the Acherousian Chersonese, where Herakles went down for Kerberos (6.2.1–2). The Heracleots gave 3,000 medimnoi of barley meal, 2,000 jars of wine, 20 oxen and 100 sheep. When the army tried to extort money from Heraclea, it split: the Arcadians and Achaeans (more than 4,000 hoplites) went their own way; Cheirisophos kept 1,400 hoplites and 700 peltasts, and Xenophon 1,700 hoplites, 300 peltasts and 40 horsemen (6.2.3–16).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Cheirisophos", "Xenophon", "Lykon of Achaea", "Kallimachos of Parrhasia"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 6.2"], "sources_modern": ["Burstein 1976", "Lee 2007"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Brownson (Loeb) brackets the list of sights at 6.2.1 as an interpolation, geographically impossible on a voyage starting at Sinope (Jason's Cape and the Thermodon, Iris and Halys lie east of it); Marchant prints it.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Heraclea Pontica", "lat": 41.283, "lon": 31.418, "role": "split of the army"}, {"name": "Acherousian Chersonese (Baba Burnu)", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 31.39, "certainty": "approximate", "role": "anchorage; Herakles' descent"}, {"name": "Jason's Cape (Yason Burnu)", "lat": 41.11, "lon": 37.68, "certainty": "probable", "role": "passed by sea"}]}, {"id": "H1-062", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Summer 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Kalpe Harbour", "summary": "The Arcadians sailed to Kalpe Harbour, halfway between Heraclea and Byzantium, and were mauled by the Bithynian Thracians. Cheirisophos marched by land; Xenophon landed at the Heracleot–Thracian border and marched inland (6.2.17–19). Reunited at Kalpe, the army lost Cheirisophos to a fever and a drug (6.4.11), beat Pharnabazos' cavalry and the Bithynians (6.5), and quarrelled with Kleandros, the Spartan governor of Byzantium (6.6).", "kind": "event", "people": ["Cheirisophos", "Neon of Asine", "Pharnabazos", "Kleandros", "Xenophon"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 6.2.17–6.6"], "sources_modern": ["Lee 2007"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kalpe Harbour (Kerpe)", "lat": 41.165, "lon": 30.215, "certainty": "probable", "role": "reunion"}, {"name": "Heracleot–Thracian border (Sangarios area)", "lat": 41.15, "lon": 30.65, "certainty": "conjectural", "role": "Xenophon's landing"}]}, {"id": "H1-063", "date": "-400", "date_text": "Autumn 400 BC (estimate)", "precision": "month", "title": "Chrysopolis and Byzantium", "summary": "The army marched through Bithynia to Chrysopolis, where it spent seven days selling its booty (6.6.37–38). Anaxibios then ferried it across to Byzantium, where the soldiers, cheated of pay, nearly sacked the city (7.1). The retreat from Cunaxa was over. The later summary at 7.8.26 gives the whole journey up and down as 215 stages, 1,150 parasangs, 34,255 stadia and 1 year 3 months (probably interpolated: Brownson's 'a late editor').", "kind": "event", "people": ["Anaxibios", "Xenophon"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 6.6.37–38, 7.1", "Anab. 7.8.26"], "sources_modern": ["Lee 2007", "Stronk 1995, The Ten Thousand in Thrace"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The totals at 7.8.26 do not agree with each other or with the time actually taken; they are usually thought to be a later addition.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chrysopolis (Üsküdar)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 29.015, "role": "booty sold"}, {"name": "Byzantium", "lat": 41.01, "lon": 28.982, "role": "end of the retreat"}]}, {"id": "H1-064", "date": "-400", "date_end": "-399", "date_text": "Winter 400/399 – spring 399 BC", "precision": "month", "title": "Service with Seuthes; handed to Thibron", "summary": "The rest of the army, about 6,000 men (Xenophon's own round figure, 7.7.23), wintered in Thrace in the pay of the Odrysian prince Seuthes. In spring 399 it crossed back to Asia and was taken into Spartan service under Thibron against the Persians (7.2–7.8). This lies outside the atlas, but it closes the story.", "kind": "event", "people": ["Seuthes II", "Thibron", "Xenophon"], "sources_primary": ["Xenophon, Anab. 7.2–7.8 (7.7.23: about 6,000)"], "sources_modern": ["Stronk 1995"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "About 6,000 is Xenophon's own round figure ('to be well spoken of by six thousand men', 7.7.23), not a muster count.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Seuthes' Thrace (Salmydessos coast)", "lat": 41.6, "lon": 28.0, "certainty": "approximate", "role": "winter service"}, {"name": "Pergamon", "lat": 39.132, "lon": 27.184, "role": "army handed over to Thibron"}]}], "turkish": [], "synthesis": "## The march\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch02_march.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch02_march.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1440\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map from Cunaxa in Mesopotamia to Byzantium, showing the route of the Ten Thousand up the Tigris, through the land of the Kardouchoi and Armenia to Trapezous, along the coast to Kotyora and by sea to Kalpe.\"></a><figcaption>The march of the Ten Thousand, 401–400 BC. Between the stages Xenophon names, the line follows the easiest ground and is ours, not his. Gymnias and Theches are drawn hollow because their sites are conjectural. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nAt Cunaxa, in September 401 BC, Cyrus the Younger was killed fighting his brother, King Artaxerxes II. His Greek mercenaries, 10,400 hoplites and 2,500 peltasts (*Anabasis* 1.7.10), were left stranded north of Babylon. When the satrap Tissaphernes seized their generals at the Great Zab, the army elected new ones, among them the Athenian Xenophon and the Spartan Cheirisophos, and marched north: up the Tigris, past the ruined Assyrian capitals that Xenophon calls Larisa and Mespila, which were Nimrud and Nineveh; through the country of the Kardouchoi, in seven days of fighting; and across the Kentrites, the Bohtan Su, into Armenia, about December.\n\nWestern Armenia meant snow a fathom deep, a north wind, frostbite and snow-blindness; in one stretch about thirty soldiers died (4.5.4). The army rested in villages of earth-houses where the people drank barley beer through straws. Then came a run of hostile peoples: the Phasianoi at the upper Araxes; the Taochoi, whose women leapt with their children from their stronghold; the Chalybes, fiercest of all; and the Skythenoi. The ruler of the city of Gymnias gave the army a guide, who promised them a sight of the sea within five days. On the fifth day, from a mountain called Theches, the vanguard shouted \"Thalatta, thalatta!\", \"The sea, the sea!\".\n\nThey came down through the land of the Makrones, where a man who had been a slave at Athens could speak their language, and beat the Colchians on a mountain. There they ate the rhododendron honey, which laid them out: those who ate a little were like drunk men, those who ate much like madmen, but no one died, and they got up on the third or fourth day (4.8.20–21). Two stages later they reached Trapezous.\n\nThey stayed at Trapezous about thirty days. They camped in Colchian villages and raided the Drilai of the mountains; they held games run by the Spartan exile Drakontios, with a horse race down the slope into the sea; and they waited in vain for Spartan ships. From Trapezous they went on by road to Kerasous, where a review counted 8,600 men (5.3.3) and a tithe was vowed to Artemis, later spent on Xenophon's estate at Skillous; then through the tower-dwelling Mossynoikoi, whose king burned in his wooden tower, and past the iron-working Chalybes and the Tibarenoi to Kotyora, where they spent 45 days. At Kotyora they quarrelled with Sinope's envoy Hekatonymos, and Xenophon toyed with founding a city on the Pontus. From there they sailed to Harmene, Sinope's harbour, where Cheirisophos was elected sole commander, and on to Heraclea, where the army split in three: more than 4,000 Arcadians and Achaeans, 1,400 hoplites and 700 peltasts with Cheirisophos, and 1,700 hoplites, 300 peltasts and 40 horsemen with Xenophon (6.2.10–16). They came together again at Kalpe Harbour, where Cheirisophos died, sold their booty at Chrysopolis, and crossed to Byzantium in the autumn of 400. About 6,000 of them, in Xenophon's own round figure, entered Spartan service in the spring of 399 (7.7.23).\n\nThe army spent about 30 days at Trapezous, 10 at Kerasous, 45 at Kotyora, 5 at Harmene and 7 at Chrysopolis. Like any invading army, it plundered the Colchians, the Drilai and the Paphlagonians, took captives and sold them, and joined one faction of the Mossynoikoi against another. Xenophon records this plainly. Neither Armenians nor Georgians appear under those names yet: Xenophon's \"Armenia\" is a Persian satrapy whose villagers spoke a language that his interpreter rendered through Persian.\n\n## The stages and distances\n\nXenophon counts the march in stages and parasangs.\n\n| Leg | Xenophon | Passage |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kentrites to a village with a palace | 1 stage, 5 parasangs | 4.4.1 |\n| past the sources of the Tigris | 2 stages, 10 | 4.4.3 |\n| to the Teleboas | 3, 15 | 4.4.3 |\n| to Tiribazos' palace | 3, 15 | 4.4.7 |\n| to the Euphrates (Murat), \"through desert country\" | 3, 15 | 4.5.2 |\n| to the underground villages, in snow | 3, 15 (13 in the Loeb text) | 4.5.3 |\n| to the Phasis | 7, 35 | 4.6.4 |\n| to the pass of the Chalybes, Taochoi and Phasianoi | 2, 10 | 4.6.5 |\n| to the Taochoi | 5, 30 | 4.7.1 |\n| to the Harpasos, through the Chalybes | 7, 50 | 4.7.15 |\n| to the Skythenian villages | 4, 20 | 4.7.18 |\n| to Gymnias | 4, 20 | 4.7.19 |\n| to Theches | 5 days, not measured | 4.7.20–21 |\n| through the Makrones to the Colchian border | 3, 10 | 4.8.1, 4.8.8 |\n| to Trapezous | 2, 7 | 4.8.22 |\n| Trapezous to Kerasous | 3 days | 5.3.2 |\n| through the Mossynoikoi | 8 stages | 5.5.1 |\n| through the Tibarenoi to Kotyora | 2 stages | 5.5.3 |\n\nFrom the Kentrites to Trapezous this adds up to 49 stages and 257 parasangs (255 in the Loeb text), with 8 days unmeasured. The total at 5.5.4, generally regarded as a later addition, gives 122 stages, 620 parasangs, 18,600 stadia and eight months from the battlefield to Kotyora; the closing summary at 7.8.26 (215 stages, 1,150 parasangs, 34,255 stadia, a year and three months) is likewise the note of a later editor. A parasang is nominally 30 stadia, about 5.5–6 km, but in the mountains Xenophon's parasangs shrink: from Theches to Trapezous the army averaged 3.4 parasangs a day. In a straight line it is about 680 km from the Kentrites to Trapezous and about 1,450 km from Cunaxa to Kotyora; the roads were longer.\n\n## Where was Theches?\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch02_theches.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch02_theches.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the mountains between Bayburt and Trapezous, with eight numbered places proposed for Theches, the route drawn in this atlas, two other proposed routes, and the coast to Kotyora.\"></a><figcaption>Where was Theches? Eight of the places proposed for the mountain from which the Greeks saw the sea, numbered as in the key; the route drawn in this atlas follows Mitford, and the dashed lines are two other proposed routes. The peoples are placed where Xenophon puts them. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nThe most recent survey, by Brennan & Tuplin (2023), lists about twenty proposals made since 1818, in four sectors; the map and the table give the main ones.\n\n| Candidate | Proposed by | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| the Kolat area, about 20 km SSW of Maçka (\"Deveboynu Tepe\", with a large cairn base) | Mitford 2000, 2018; Manfredi, who changed his view | the leading contender; on the later Roman summer road |\n| about 4 km east of the Zigana pass | Lendle 1995; Chesney 1850, Lehmann-Haupt, Gassner | the classic view |\n| south of Toplukdüzü Tepe, NW of Torul | Manfredi 1986 | later abandoned by him |\n| İskobel | local tradition | central sector |\n| the NW spur of Polut Dağı (2,315 m) | Brennan & Tuplin 2023 | \"viable\", not demonstrable |\n| Madur Dağı (2,742 m) | Bilgin 2000; Winfield 1977; Brennan 2012 | eastern sector |\n| Vavuk, Kop, Gavur Dağı, near Gümüşhane | 19th-century travellers (Ainsworth, Chesney, Kinneir) | southern sector; outdated |\n\nBrennan & Tuplin conclude that \"the thalatta spot must remain a matter for conjecture\". Several things make the problem hard: Xenophon's narrative is shaped as a story, not as an itinerary; the five-day leg from Gymnias has no measured distance; Diodorus calls the mountain Chenion and puts it fifteen days from Gymnias, usually taken as a copying error; and in spring haze the sea is seldom visible. Gymnias itself is usually placed at Bayburt, or somewhere between Bayburt and Gümüşhane.\n\n## The dates\n\nXenophon gives no calendar dates, and every date is a modern reconstruction: Cunaxa in September 401; the snow march in December 401 and January 400; Theches in January or February 400 in older handbooks, in spring in most recent commentators; Trapezous in spring 400; Kotyora in early summer; Byzantium in the autumn of 400; and Thibron's Spartan army in the spring of 399.\n\n## The sources\n\nXenophon's *Anabasis* is the account of the march by one of its generals, with the stages and parasangs of each leg, the peoples met and the life of the army. Diodorus (14.19–31) gives a shorter parallel account from Ephorus. Bessarion's *Encomium of Trebizond*, of the 1430s, retells the welcome at Trapezous, adding that the city took the army within its walls and saw it safely on to Sinope; in Xenophon the army camps in Colchian villages. Among modern works, Brennan & Tuplin (2023) review every proposal for Theches, with a full catalogue; Mitford (2000, 2018) makes the leading proposal from fieldwork; Lendle's commentary (1995) is the standard topographical guide; Manfredi (1986) studies the whole route; Lee (2007) the life of the army; Waterfield (2006) gives a narrative; the volumes edited by Lane Fox (2004) and Tuplin (2004) treat the work and its author; and Bilgin (2000) proposes Madur Dağı.\n"};
